<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Trzebnica</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Trebnitz</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Trzebnica is a town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-silesia">Lower Silesia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wroclaw">Wrocław</a>. In 1202 Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-bearded">Henry the Bearded</a> and his wife Hedwig founded a Cistercian convent of nuns there. Hedwig lived her last years at the convent and was buried in its church, which became a major pilgrimage site after her canonisation in 1267.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/trzebnica.jpg" alt="Trzebnica" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: s_mile · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Trzebnica,_Ratusz_-_fotopolska.eu_(255217).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Trzebnica, in German Trebnitz, is a town in the Lower Silesian Voivodeship in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, in the Trzebnickie Hills about 6 km north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wroclaw">Wrocław</a>, and part of the Wrocław metropolitan area. It was an early medieval religious and trading centre, possibly more important than Wrocław. It is first mentioned in a deed of 1138, when it was held by the Polish voivode Piotr Włostowic and later seized by the Silesian duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wladyslaw-ii-the-exile">Władysław II the Exile</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-bearded">Henry I the Bearded</a> and his wife <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hedwig-of-silesia">Hedwig of Andechs</a> founded the first Cistercian convent in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> at Trzebnica, settled with German nuns from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bamberg">Bamberg</a>. In 1218 Hedwig's daughter Gertrude became abbess, the first of many Piast princesses to hold the office. Henry died in 1238 and was buried in the church; his widow moved to the convent, where she died in October 1243 and was buried too. She was canonised in 1267. The town received town privileges in 1250 and became a Bohemian fief in 1328. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hussites">Hussites</a> plundered it in 1430, and fires struck in 1456, 1505, 1511 and 1534. In 1742 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> ceded Trzebnica with most of Silesia to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, and the monastery was secularised in 1810.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The former convent church is the Sanctuary of St. Jadwiga, a regional pilgrimage site since the 14th century and a listed Historic Monument of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. The town was heavily damaged in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vistula">Vistula</a>–Oder Offensive of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> in the last days of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a> and came under Polish administration under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potsdam-agreement">Potsdam Agreement</a>, when the remaining German population was expelled. From April to June 1945 it was the first post-war regional capital of the Lower Silesian Voivodeship.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Trzebnica” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Henry I the Bearded and his wife Saint Hedwig invited German settlers, founded towns under German law and monasteries such as <b>Trzebnica</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-silesia">Duchy of Silesia</a></li></ul></section>
